  <article class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>1. Understand AI’s Capabilities and Limits</h2>
      <p>AI are powerful agents, but they’re not magic. Learn what they can and can’t do. AI excels at analyzing large datasets, automating repetitive tasks, and generating predictions, but it lacks human nuance, empathy, and strategic thinking. Leaders must know where AI adds value and where human insight still reigns supreme. Always question the output, double-check, and triple-check it. You may be surprised to learn that the answers are made up entirely or are simply incorrect.</p>
      <h2>2. Focus on the Problem First, Not the Tool or Agent</h2>
      <p>Don’t chase trends. Identify the specific challenge you’re solving before selecting or investing in AI. Start by asking: What’s the business outcome we want? Then explore how AI could assist, if at all.</p>
      <h2>3. Empower Your Teams, Don’t Replace Them</h2>
      <p>AI should augment human work, not eliminate it. Use it to handle the repetitive and time-consuming tasks, freeing your team to focus on strategic, creative, and interpersonal work.</p>
      <h2>4. Encourage Responsible AI Use</h2>
      <p>Set clear guidelines for ethical AI use. Emphasize transparency, data privacy, and avoiding bias. Encourage your teams to think critically about the outputs AI generates.</p>
      <h2>5. Train Continuously</h2>
      <p>Provide regular training for your team on AI tools relevant to their roles. The landscape changes fast; build a culture of curiosity and learning. We can help you with this at <a href="/workshops">futureforth.com</a>.</p>
      <h2>6. Stay Human-Centered</h2>
      <p>Always keep people at the core. AI should make work more meaningful, not more robotic. Lead with empathy and focus on how technology can improve human experiences at work.</p>
      <h2>7. Start Small and Iterate</h2>
      <p>Pilot AI agents in small, low-risk areas before rolling them out more broadly. Track results and gather feedback to refine how you implement solutions.</p>
      <h2>8. Collaborate Across Departments</h2>
      <p>AI isn’t just an IT or data team initiative. Engage HR, marketing, operations, and others to ensure tools address real needs and integrate seamlessly.</p>
      <h2>9. Lead by Example</h2>
      <p>Use AI agents yourself. Leaders who engage with AI personally, whether by revising emails or using predictive insights, show adaptability and build a culture of innovation.</p>
      <h2>10. Stay Curious and Keep Experimenting</h2>
      <p>AI is evolving daily. The best leaders don’t pretend to have all the answers they stay curious, ask good questions, and encourage their teams to test, learn, and adapt.</p>
      <h2>Final Takeaway</h2>
